  task_events    id, task_id, actor_id, type, old_value, new_value, created_at
  notifications  id, user_id, event_id, read_at?, created_at
                 UNIQUE (user_id, event_id)

  Four decisions in there are load-bearing, and they're the ones I'd want you to push back on now rather than later:

  [1m[38;5;153mtask_watchers.source[0m — one of [38;5;153mimplicit_assignee[39m, [38;5;153mimplicit_creator[39m, [38;5;153mexplicit[39m. Without this column, reassigning a task
  either strips a watch the user deliberately added, or leaves the old assignee subscribed forever. With it, removing an
  implicit subscription can't clobber an explicit one. This is the single subtlest part of the whole design and it's
  cheap now and painful to retrofit.

  [1m[38;5;153mold_value[39m / [38;5;153mnew_value[39m as text on one events table[0m, rather than three typed tables. The feed has to render "moved from
  In Progress to Done" correctly [3meven after the task has changed twice more[0m — so the event has to carry its own values
  rather than reading the task's current state. One table with text values keeps the feed query a single join.

  [1m[38;5;153mUNIQUE (user_id, event_id)[0m — makes fan-out idempotent. A retried or double-fired write can't produce duplicate feed
  items, which means the fan-out path doesn't need a transaction spanning event-insert and notification-insert to be
  safe.

  [1mThe actor is excluded from their own fan-out.[0m You don't get notified about your own edits. Obvious, universally
  forgotten, and it's a filter at fan-out time rather than at read time so the unread count stays a plain [38;5;153mCOUNT(*)[39m.

  Two semantics that follow, worth confirming: unwatching a task leaves your existing notifications in place — they were
  already yours — it only stops new ones. And setting [38;5;153mdue_at[39m from null counts as a due-date change, same as moving it.
